## Lore

After the Great Cataclysm, the survivors of Japan read the return of the old gods and spirits as a sign that they had lost their balance with the world, and turned from technology back to tradition to build the New Empire. The samurai lead it: warrior scholars, the elite of a military government, mounted knights, administrators and landowners whose families trace their lines back centuries. Only the samurai caste has the right to carry weapons openly; peasants and eta may not, and outsiders who go armed are watched closely. The daisho is the badge of the samurai, the longbow a traditional favorite, and most are skilled in at least two other weapons. The top-knot marks the samurai; a shaved head usually means a period of religious retreat.

## The Bushido Code of the New Empire

Bushido sets the samurai apart through honor: complete loyalty to the shogun or daimyo, honesty, courage, politeness and self-control. The five virtues (gojo) are Sincerity and Honor (repay one''s giri, keep one''s word at any cost, hold to one''s principles), Wisdom (common sense; beware magic, the supernatural and technology; do not abandon an objective), Propriety (duty, self-control, courtesy, contempt for money, avoiding the technology that destroyed Japan, respect for authority, fighting with honor, avenging the wronged), Benevolence (kindness, no needless cruelty or torture, swift justice and swift mercy, protection of the innocent) and Courage (a fearless spirit, meeting death as a warrior, facing one''s fears, defending one''s lord and the weak).

The five relationships (gorin), in order: samurai to lord (above even Bushido), father and son or teacher and pupil, husband and wife, juniors and seniors, and friends. A samurai may still challenge and replace a tyrannical or cruel lord without breaking the code. Ritual suicide is an accepted way to save face, but not while one owes a debt.

## Samurai Armor

The New Empire gives its samurai a suit of magic lamellar armor with heavy shoulder plates and a face mask that may be human, demonic or animal; it is made in ceremonies by priests, sorcerers or dragons, inherited, taken from oni, or given by the gods, and monks sometimes give armor of Millennium Tree bark. 100 M.D.C. is typical, some as much as 180; it is not environmental.

## Samurai Family Background

Roll percentile dice. 01-10 Noble, of the old samurai ruling class (a pair of greater-weapon daisho, 1D4x100+400 years old); 11-20 new nobility, minor landowner and farmer; 21-30 new nobility, wealthy landowner in farming, livestock and administration; 31-37 a peasant farmer who proved a warrior born; 38-47 new nobility, military; 48-52 medicine; 53-62 scholastic; 63-70 business owner; 71-77 political; 78-83 religious; 84-91 science; 92-00 a new samurai risen from mercenary or ronin.

Family skills: three picks at +10% in addition to O.C.C. skills. Old nobility: domestic (bonsai, go, poetry, dance, sing) and/or technical. Minor landowner: wilderness or physical. Wealthy landowner: math and botany from science, and/or wilderness or domestic. Military: espionage and/or weapon proficiencies. Medicine: medical and/or science. Scholar: science or technical. Business owner: math and archaeology from science, and/or rogue or domestic. Politics: language, literacy and writing from technical, and/or domestic or rogue. Religious: art, language, literacy, lore and mythology from technical, and/or domestic. Science: science. New samurai: rogue or wilderness.

## Female Samurai

Women of samurai clans are trained for combat too, in kendo rather than zanji shinjinken-ryo, with spear, sword and paired weapons, and they carry the daisho but do not use the bow. Their Special Training is Samurai Horsemanship only, the Daisho and the Chi Death Blow as the male''s, a P.P.E. of P.E. x3 +1D6 per level, and the male''s other bonuses plus kendo''s. The code, skills and all else are the male''s. Most took up arms to defend family, home and land or to avenge slain kin; some are born warriors who fight the oni for the Empire.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'true-samurai');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'true-samurai';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-true-samurai-class.sql');
